import os, sys
out = sys.argv[1]
head = '''You are making game art for "Pause" (Unity, vertical 2D shooter, phone portrait, the player flies UP the screen). Work ONLY inside this directory tree; do not edit any .cs/.meta/.json or any existing file; do not delete anything; do not commit.
New files go ONLY to ./Pause/Assets/Art/Resources/ShipArt/Hulls/src~/voltviper/codex/ (the trailing ~ matters: Unity ignores it). Put helpers in its tools/ subfolder.
Read ./docs/art-style.md first (neon pixel art). The player ship is "VoltViper", redrawn in hi-res neon pixel art (azure blue armour, hexagonal teal canopy, gunmetal engine legs, cream wing bolt lights, red-and-white wingtip diamonds, small red nose winglets).

REFERENCES (attached images, in order): (1) skinref_state0.png, (2) skinref_state1.png, (3) skinref_state2.png: the FINAL approved flipbook, three 3 x 3 sheets (intact, damaged, wreck), 9 poses each in the order key pose / bob up / bob up lights off / glint 1 / glint 2 lights off / bob down / bank left / bank right / hit flash. Each is 1536 x 1536 (cells 512 px).
RULES: no pink or magenta anywhere. No flames. No outline or halo around the ship. Flat pure #00FF00 background.
'''
body = '''
JOB SKIN-{name}: a COLOURWAY of the finished flipbook. Redraw each of the three sheets in the "{name}" colourway and write skin_{name}_state0.png, skin_{name}_state1.png, skin_{name}_state2.png (same 3 x 3 layout, each cell 1024 x 1024 i.e. 3072 x 3072 per file, flat #00FF00 background, every pose at the SAME position and scale as the reference sheet). THE DRAWING MUST STAY IDENTICAL cell for cell (same silhouette, panel lines, damage, cracks, glints, bank perspective, light dashes): ONLY COLOURS CHANGE:
{rules}
Everything else (canopy glass, engine nozzle glow, ink edge, glints, and ALL damage effects: sparks, glowing orange tears, embers, soot) stays as in the reference. The hit-flash cell stays bone-white.
Use IMAGE GENERATION to repaint each sheet from the reference (several candidates, pick the truest to the reference), then clean with nearest-neighbour. Do NOT simply hue-shift with a script: the result must be a painted redraw, but pixel-faithful to the drawing (keep the same plating texture, panel lines and rim lights, just in the new colours with proper ramp shading).
Write tools/check_skin_{name}.py that, for each of the three files and each of the 9 cells, prints: silhouette (non-green mask) difference vs the reference cell (must be < 1.5%), difference over the canopy/teal region (must be < 6%), and the fraction of all pixels that changed (must be > {chg}% so it is visibly a new colourway). FIX AND REDRAW until every number passes; print the summary in your final message. Finish with honest caveats.
'''
skins = {
    'Night': ('''- the azure ARMOUR (all blue plating, with its darker shading and light-blue highlights) becomes very dark midnight indigo-navy metal, clearly darker and more violet-navy than the stock blue: base #2A2E6B, shadow #1A1F45, rim-light highlights bright cyan #6EF2EE along the upper-left edges of the plates (neon edge lighting) and a few pale blue-white highlights. Keep the cream wing bolt lights, the wingtip diamonds and the red nose winglets as they are. The teal canopy stays teal.''', 12),
    'Sodium': ('''- the azure ARMOUR becomes warm sodium-lamp ORANGE metal: base #F2862B, shadow #A9481A, highlights #FFB43C with pale yellow-white hot edges. Keep the cream wing bolt lights and the wingtip diamonds as they are; the red nose winglets become deep brown-red so they still read against orange.''', 12),
    'Bone': ('''- the azure ARMOUR becomes warm BONE-white ceramic armour: base #F4EAD4, shadow #B9AE98, highlights near-white, with plate seams in a slightly darker warm grey. The cream wing bolt lights, the wingtip diamond lights and the central star mark become bright electric BLUE (#2E9BE6 with a hot #8FD3FF core) so they still read; the red nose winglets stay red.''', 12),
    'Storm': ('''- this is the special STORM LIVERY: keep the azure armour exactly as it is and paint on it three crisp, sharp-edged amber CHEVRONS, each a clean "^" arrowhead shape (two straight diagonal arms of equal length meeting at a pointed apex that points UP toward the nose, arms rising at roughly 35 degrees from horizontal; each arm band about 7% of the ship width thick), flat amber #FFB43C with a one-pixel-step orange #F2862B lower edge and a one-pixel cream #F4EAD4 highlight on the top edge. Place them stacked on the rear half of the central fuselage, centred on the centre line, below the star mark and above the engine legs: apex of the first at about 62% of the ship height from the top, the other two 6% and 12% of the height below it, each spanning about 38% of the ship width (arms ending at the fuselage edges), the lowest narrowest. Also paint one smaller crisp "^" chevron (about 14% of the ship width wide) on each wing, same orientation as the fuselage ones: apex UP, arms down-left and down-right, centred on the wing plating just inboard of the bolt light, not covering the bolt light or tip diamond. The chevrons are part of the hull plating, so in the damaged and wreck sheets they follow the damage: cracked, scorched or missing where the damage cuts through, and in the bank cells they are foreshortened with the plates they sit on. Nothing else on the ship changes colour (so the fraction changed is smaller here).''', 3),
}
for k, (rules, chg) in skins.items():
    open(os.path.join(out, 'skin_%s.md' % k), 'w').write(head + body.format(name=k, rules=rules, chg=chg))
